1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:03,400
Friend: Trust me?

2
00:00:09,167 --> 00:00:09,947
Friend: Half?

3
00:00:09,967 --> 00:00:10,967
Coffee seller: Half.

4
00:00:11,133 --> 00:00:13,453
So what if an AI predicted every choice
you make,

5
00:00:13,473 --> 00:00:17,113
and your life got cheaper every time you
did what it predicted?

6
00:00:17,767 --> 00:00:20,047
That card over your head is your tag: what
you'll do next.

7
00:00:20,067 --> 00:00:21,827
Do it, and your rent can fall to half.

8
00:00:21,847 --> 00:00:24,047
Don't, and your tag goes red where
everyone can see it,

9
00:00:24,247 --> 00:00:25,247
and your prices climb.

10
00:00:25,933 --> 00:00:30,973
Closing time. A food court in Singapore.
You and your best friend run this stall.

11
00:00:30,993 --> 00:00:35,633
Every night you count the cash box. Every
night he holds out his hand for the key,

12
00:00:35,653 --> 00:00:37,053
like he's going to run off with the money.

13
00:00:37,433 --> 00:00:41,853
Every night you hold it over his palm, and
pocket it. The oldest joke you two have.

14
00:00:41,873 --> 00:00:44,293
Tonight the joke came with a number.

15
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:48,620
And here's the thing. It's right ninety-
nine times in a hundred. About everybody.

16
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:50,300
Here's how it works.

17
00:00:50,440 --> 00:00:54,720
Over every head, a tag: what you'll do in
the next ten seconds, and how sure it is.

18
00:00:54,740 --> 00:00:57,140
The city watches from every lamppost.

19
00:00:57,767 --> 00:01:02,587
Under each lamp, a board with your rate. A
hundred is full price. Fifty is half.

20
00:01:02,607 --> 00:01:04,887
Do what your tag says, and your rate
drops.

21
00:01:04,907 --> 00:01:08,827
Your coffee, your fare, your rent, all
cheaper. It knows you.

22
00:01:08,847 --> 00:01:11,487
Ninety-nine times in a hundred, it knows
you.

23
00:01:11,967 --> 00:01:14,507
Go against your tag, and it turns red, and
your rate climbs.

24
00:01:14,527 --> 00:01:18,347
Red means trouble, and everyone can see
it. One more thing.

25
00:01:18,367 --> 00:01:20,747
It only knows the choices you've already
made.

26
00:01:20,767 --> 00:01:24,527
It has never seen you do something new.
Remember that.

27
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:28,880
Friend: Oh, come on.

28
00:01:29,533 --> 00:01:33,273
Tear it off, and the next lamp prints
another. It saw that coming too.

29
00:01:33,867 --> 00:01:36,227
He hums all the way home. He stops. It saw
that too.

30
00:01:36,933 --> 00:01:38,373
Friend: It likes me.

31
00:01:39,033 --> 00:01:40,853
Nobody here finds a price on a choice
strange.

32
00:01:40,873 --> 00:01:46,333
This city has billed drivers for its
busiest roads by time of day since 1998.

33
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:50,960
Your rate starts at a hundred. Tomorrow
you'll find out which way it moves.

34
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:54,840
Morning. You've worked it out. It guesses.
So guess wrong.

35
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:04,580
You beat it four times. Four reds, and
every red costs you.

36
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:07,600
Your rate's up a quarter, before
breakfast.

37
00:02:07,620 --> 00:02:09,700
And on the fifth try, it's caught up.

38
00:02:10,167 --> 00:02:13,947
A professor once wrote a tiny program that
guessed which of two keys people would

39
00:02:13,967 --> 00:02:15,587
press when they tried to type at random.

40
00:02:15,607 --> 00:02:20,007
Right seventy to eighty per cent of the
time. He couldn't beat it himself.

41
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:21,980
Trying to be random is a pattern.

42
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:25,400
So your coffee this morning costs more
than it did last night.

43
00:02:25,867 --> 00:02:26,867
Coffee seller: You're up today.

44
00:02:26,967 --> 00:02:28,767
Fine. Let a coin choose.

45
00:02:32,133 --> 00:02:33,213
Friend: It's in my head.

46
00:02:33,833 --> 00:02:37,353
A coin is random. Reaching for it isn't.
The tag doesn't guess the coin.

47
00:02:37,373 --> 00:02:40,093
It guesses him, reaching for it again.

48
00:02:40,767 --> 00:02:44,407
Problem one. Fighting your tag costs
money, and it learns how you fight.

49
00:02:44,427 --> 00:02:48,667
So you stop fighting yours. And start
reading everyone else's.

50
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:53,680
Lunch. You read the tags over the queue,
and serve each plate before they ask.

51
00:02:58,800 --> 00:02:59,800
Coffee seller: Show-off.

52
00:02:59,967 --> 00:03:03,807
Nobody waits. Their tags come true, yours
comes true, and your rate drops.

53
00:03:04,447 --> 00:03:08,207
The queue doubles by the weekend, and the
court next door starts reading tags too.

54
00:03:08,867 --> 00:03:11,327
The landlord's letter. A low rate means
low rent.

55
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Landlord: Keep it up.

56
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:14,280
Friend: Half!

57
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:16,820
Half rent on the stall. And a thought you
can't put down.

58
00:03:16,840 --> 00:03:20,520
If you can read your customers' tags, you
can read anyone's.

59
00:03:20,933 --> 00:03:25,313
The platform. A hood behind a woman's bag,
and a red tag: takes wallet.

60
00:03:25,753 --> 00:03:27,793
The first time the city has forecast a
crime.

61
00:03:31,033 --> 00:03:32,193
Pickpocket: I didn't touch it.

62
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:37,600
Call the police? He hasn't done anything
yet. A forecast isn't a crime.

63
00:03:38,267 --> 00:03:43,047
Every head turns anyway. Doors shut on
him. The coffee seller won't pour.

64
00:03:43,067 --> 00:03:46,747
A red tag is a verdict everyone can read,
and nobody asks for the trial.

65
00:03:47,347 --> 00:03:50,567
By the weekend he's afraid to move at all.
His tag says stands still.

66
00:03:50,587 --> 00:03:52,367
Safest man on the platform.

67
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,300
The kid with the cap plays rock-paper-
scissors for the seat by the door.

68
00:03:56,320 --> 00:03:59,860
Wins with rock. His tag says rock again.

69
00:03:59,880 --> 00:04:03,620
So the man beside him throws paper, every
time.

70
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:05,200
Kid: No fair.

71
00:04:05,833 --> 00:04:08,073
People who win tend to play the same thing
again.

72
00:04:08,733 --> 00:04:09,733
Magician: Pick a card.

73
00:04:10,133 --> 00:04:11,713
His tag says where the queen is hiding.

74
00:04:11,733 --> 00:04:16,193
Nobody stays for a trick they've already
seen. By lunch, no act.

75
00:04:16,667 --> 00:04:19,547
Now the whole city is reading tags. The
penalty kick.

76
00:04:19,567 --> 00:04:22,267
Tag says left, keeper goes left.

77
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:27,160
Bet on it? Everyone can read the tag, so
everyone bets the same way.

78
00:04:27,820 --> 00:04:30,620
The bookie read it first, and pays back
only what you put in.

79
00:04:31,167 --> 00:04:34,207
Kick against your tag and your rate
climbs, so every striker obeys.

80
00:04:34,227 --> 00:04:37,107
Every keeper reads it. Nobody scores.

81
00:04:37,733 --> 00:04:39,033
Newsreader: Another nil-nil.

82
00:04:39,733 --> 00:04:42,613
Phone records from fifty thousand people
once showed this.

83
00:04:42,633 --> 00:04:43,913
Where a person will be is, in principle,

84
00:04:43,933 --> 00:04:46,393
guessable up to about ninety-three per
cent of the time.

85
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:49,980
You thought you were a free spirit. You're
a timetable.

86
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:55,240
The morning train. Forty heads. Forty
tags, the same three words.

87
00:04:55,260 --> 00:04:56,560
Nobody looks out of the window.

88
00:04:59,733 --> 00:05:01,953
To live cheap here, you have to be easy to
guess.

89
00:05:01,973 --> 00:05:07,173
So here's problem two: the cheapest people
in this city never surprise anyone.

90
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:12,760
The man with the same lunch. Same table,
same minute, same dish, for a year.

91
00:05:13,140 --> 00:05:14,140
Lowest rate in the city.

92
00:05:14,767 --> 00:05:16,607
Friend: That's the champion?

93
00:05:17,367 --> 00:05:21,707
People farm it. In 1902, Hanoi paid a
bounty for every rat tail.

94
00:05:21,727 --> 00:05:25,167
Officials found people breeding rats for
the tails.

95
00:05:25,187 --> 00:05:28,267
Pay for predictability, and people breed
it.

96
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:30,560
On the street, a man sells boring.

97
00:05:31,267 --> 00:05:34,287
Coach: Same route. Same lunch. Same face.

98
00:05:35,133 --> 00:05:38,073
Your rate falls. Sixty-one. Fifty-five.

99
00:05:38,093 --> 00:05:39,993
You've learned to read your tag before you
reach.

100
00:05:40,633 --> 00:05:45,133
Quiet all week. Then tonight, the landlord
impression. The napkin tie.

101
00:05:51,107 --> 00:05:52,307
Friend: It knows the bit.

102
00:05:52,900 --> 00:05:56,200
He stops doing his bits. What's a joke
when the tag says it first?

103
00:05:56,220 --> 00:06:00,660
That's what the discount really costs. Not
money. Surprises.

104
00:06:01,533 --> 00:06:03,353
And the city's board, how often the tags
come true,

105
00:06:03,373 --> 00:06:08,473
says ninety-nine point five now. Every
week it's right more often.

106
00:06:08,493 --> 00:06:11,313
Is it getting smarter? Hold that thought.

107
00:06:11,833 --> 00:06:14,253
You thought it read minds. Nothing reads
minds.

108
00:06:14,967 --> 00:06:20,107
In 2008, brain scans could guess whether a
person would press a left or a right

109
00:06:20,127 --> 00:06:23,307
button a few seconds before they felt
they'd chosen.

110
00:06:23,327 --> 00:06:26,527
The guess was right about sixty per cent
of the time. A coin gets fifty.

111
00:06:26,933 --> 00:06:31,513
Sixty. And the tag over you says ninety-
nine. So where does the rest come from?

112
00:06:31,533 --> 00:06:33,893
Keep that question. We'll answer it.

113
00:06:34,300 --> 00:06:38,920
And your best friend never stopped
fighting his tag. The coin. The torn tags.

114
00:06:38,940 --> 00:06:41,900
The jokes he wouldn't drop. Every red cost
him.

115
00:06:41,920 --> 00:06:44,020
His rate climbs, and his rent with it.

116
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:46,040
He's been staring at the cash box all
week.

117
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:48,340
Friend: Machine hates me.

118
00:06:49,100 --> 00:06:52,000
Tomorrow his hand comes out for the key,
like every night.

119
00:06:52,020 --> 00:06:54,100
And this time the tag won't think it's a
joke.

120
00:06:54,767 --> 00:06:58,287
Closing. The court half dark. His hand
comes out.

121
00:07:02,333 --> 00:07:03,333
Friend: Trust me?

122
00:07:07,100 --> 00:07:09,280
Friend: Machine's drunk.

123
00:07:10,133 --> 00:07:15,493
His tag says he'll take the key, for real.
Seventy-one per cent sure.

124
00:07:15,513 --> 00:07:18,013
You've put that key in your pocket a
thousand times as a joke.

125
00:07:18,033 --> 00:07:21,713
Tonight it's the first time you've locked
something away from him.

126
00:07:22,233 --> 00:07:23,233
Coffee seller: Not with that tag.

127
00:07:23,900 --> 00:07:25,720
His coffee's gone. His rent doubles.

128
00:07:26,120 --> 00:07:28,920
And the whole platform would rather sit
next to the pickpocket.

129
00:07:29,433 --> 00:07:30,593
Pickpocket: Plenty of room, everyone.

130
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:35,220
But the tag doesn't say thief. It says
seventy-one.

131
00:07:35,240 --> 00:07:36,580
So who gets a red tag like that?

132
00:07:37,100 --> 00:07:41,280
In America, a risk score used in courts
wrongly flagged Black defendants as future

133
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:44,120
criminals at almost twice the rate of
white defendants.

134
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:47,940
And a crowd of strangers online, given two
lines about each defendant,

135
00:07:47,960 --> 00:07:51,960
predicted who'd reoffend about as well as
the software did.

136
00:07:52,667 --> 00:07:54,427
He's broke, and his rent just doubled.

137
00:07:54,447 --> 00:07:57,267
That's all the machine sees: a broke man
next to a cash box.

138
00:07:57,287 --> 00:08:00,407
It isn't seeing a thief. It's seeing a man
in trouble.

139
00:08:00,427 --> 00:08:03,827
And it can't tell the two apart. Neither
can the food court.

140
00:08:04,367 --> 00:08:08,707
Every night his number climbs. Seventy-
one. Eighty. Eighty-eight.

141
00:08:08,727 --> 00:08:11,827
Tonight your tag says count the box,
alone. You don't.

142
00:08:15,933 --> 00:08:17,653
Friend: That cost you.

143
00:08:18,467 --> 00:08:21,027
Rent's due tomorrow. Both your rents, in
one tin box.

144
00:08:21,467 --> 00:08:24,547
And that's when his tag says the thing the
whole court has been waiting for.

145
00:08:25,300 --> 00:08:29,440
Closing. The last customers gone. The
coffee seller's phone is already up.

146
00:08:29,460 --> 00:08:32,600
Because his tag says takes the box.
Ninety-four.

147
00:08:33,533 --> 00:08:37,273
Everything it ever got right about you was
small. A coffee, a train, a key.

148
00:08:37,593 --> 00:08:40,573
When scientists watched people make a
choice that mattered,

149
00:08:40,593 --> 00:08:42,353
where real charity money went,

150
00:08:42,373 --> 00:08:46,033
the early brain signal they used to
predict small choices was missing.

151
00:08:46,633 --> 00:08:49,953
It has never seen you choose something
that mattered. And you're about to.

152
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:53,720
A month of half price. What did you stop
doing for it?

153
00:08:54,133 --> 00:08:58,013
Pocket the key, and your tag comes true,
and your rent stays half.

154
00:08:58,033 --> 00:09:01,233
His tag says he'll take the box. So what
if you just give it to him?

155
00:09:01,253 --> 00:09:04,673
Hand him the key, and your tag goes red in
front of everyone.

156
00:09:05,033 --> 00:09:06,233
And your rent goes back to full.

157
00:09:06,667 --> 00:09:10,827
Remember? It only knows what you've done
before. So do something you've never done.

158
00:09:11,400 --> 00:09:12,400
Ninety-four.

159
00:09:26,467 --> 00:09:30,567
The first blank card the city has ever
made. His tag said he'd steal the box.

160
00:09:30,587 --> 00:09:32,387
Now it says nothing.

161
00:09:33,200 --> 00:09:34,200
Friend: I was going to.

162
00:09:38,467 --> 00:09:39,467
Kid: Do it again.

163
00:09:39,800 --> 00:09:41,960
You can't steal what you've just been
handed.

164
00:09:41,980 --> 00:09:43,920
And the machine had no card for that.

165
00:09:44,533 --> 00:09:47,653
Two red tags. You broke yours, and he did
the opposite of his.

166
00:09:47,673 --> 00:09:49,613
Every face in the court turned toward
them.

167
00:09:49,633 --> 00:09:52,833
And on the pillar, for the first time, the
city's board flaps down.

168
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:56,920
It knows you. Ninety-nine times in a
hundred, it knows you.

169
00:09:56,940 --> 00:10:01,000
That's what everyone thought. But it was
never reading you.

170
00:10:01,020 --> 00:10:03,120
You were reading it, and doing what it
said.

171
00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:07,760
Drivers tracked by their insurer for a
discount braked hard about a fifth less

172
00:10:07,780 --> 00:10:11,660
often. Within six months. The watching
changed the driving.

173
00:10:12,333 --> 00:10:16,213
An economist put it in 1975: lean on a
number to control people,

174
00:10:16,233 --> 00:10:18,273
and the number stops behaving.

175
00:10:19,067 --> 00:10:26,687
Run the board backwards. Ninety-nine.
Ninety-three. Eighty-four. Seventy-one.

176
00:10:26,707 --> 00:10:30,807
The night it started, it was right seven
times in ten. It didn't get smarter.

177
00:10:30,827 --> 00:10:34,147
You got easier to guess, because being
easy to guess made you cheaper.

178
00:10:34,167 --> 00:10:36,067
That's where the rest came from. You.

179
00:10:36,633 --> 00:10:41,613
A hundred and sixty teams once tried to
predict how children's lives would turn

180
00:10:41,633 --> 00:10:43,753
out. Thousands of facts on every family.

181
00:10:43,773 --> 00:10:47,793
The best guesses were only slightly better
than a simple one from four facts.

182
00:10:47,813 --> 00:10:50,393
People are hard to predict. Unless they're
paid to be.

183
00:10:50,800 --> 00:10:54,080
It never got better at seeing you. You got
better at being seen.

184
00:10:54,933 --> 00:10:59,553
A sociologist named it in 1948: a false
belief can make itself true,

185
00:10:59,573 --> 00:11:03,133
and the prophet points to what happened as
proof he was right all along.

186
00:11:03,153 --> 00:11:07,153
Predicting a comet, he noted, doesn't
change its orbit.

187
00:11:07,700 --> 00:11:11,480
Predicting a comet doesn't move the comet.
Predicting a person does.

188
00:11:12,067 --> 00:11:16,277
A city of teal lights. Under every one,
someone doing what the card says.

189
00:11:16,297 --> 00:11:18,367
And one lamp that just got dimmer.

190
00:11:18,967 --> 00:11:21,767
Newsreader: A blank forecast, filmed at a
food court.

191
00:11:22,467 --> 00:11:25,907
By breakfast, everyone's copying you. Hand
over a key, and save half.

192
00:11:30,033 --> 00:11:31,513
Friend: That was ours.

193
00:11:32,333 --> 00:11:34,533
By lunch the tags predict that too: hands
over key.

194
00:11:34,553 --> 00:11:38,373
It watched a thousand people do it. Your
surprise is just another habit now.

195
00:11:38,933 --> 00:11:40,433
Newsreader: Confidence is back to ninety-
nine.

196
00:11:41,367 --> 00:11:42,367
Landlord: Full rent.

197
00:11:42,433 --> 00:11:45,133
Your tags stay red. Red means full price.

198
00:11:46,100 --> 00:11:47,660
Coffee seller: Full price. Same?

199
00:11:48,367 --> 00:11:48,427
The train.

200
00:11:48,447 --> 00:11:53,927
Two red tags in the middle, paying full
fare, and every head sneaking a look.

201
00:11:59,200 --> 00:12:01,300
Friend: Trust me?

202
00:12:02,133 --> 00:12:03,133
You never look up.

203
00:12:03,467 --> 00:12:06,367
Except one. The kid with the cap. Nothing
on his card.

204
00:12:06,387 --> 00:12:10,987
Whatever he does next, the machine can't
guess it. And the city hasn't noticed yet.

205
00:12:11,567 --> 00:12:15,347
So, would you pay full price to make your
card go blank? Tell us in the comments.

206
00:12:15,367 --> 00:12:17,807
And if you would, hit like and subscribe.
